      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this OIML certificate carefully.\n\nDocument structure:\n- Page 1: Certificate header info\n  - Certificate No: R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02, Revision 1\n  - Scheme A\n  - Member State: Czech Republic\n  - Issuing Authority: Czech Metrology Institute, Okružní 31, 638 00 Brno, Czech Republic\n  - Person responsible: Jan Kalandra\n  - Applicant: ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA, Via F.lli Gracchi, 27; Cinisello Balsamo; 20 099 Italy\n  - Manufacturer: Hemina SPA, Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD; PC: 350 44, Italy\n  - Certified types: MS2500 / MV110, MS2500 / MV145\n  - Module designations: MS2500 / MV110 HV, MS2500 / MV110 LV, MS2500 / MV110 LLV, MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery, MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery, MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\n  - Recommendation: OIML R 49, Edition 2013, accuracy classes 1 and 2\n  - Page total: 15 pages\n  - Date issued: 13 September 2022 (from page 2, signature date). Revision 1 date. Actually revision 0 issued 26 May 2022, revision 1 13 September 2022. The date_issued should probably be 2022-09-13 (the current certificate revision date).\n\n- Page 2: Type evaluation report and test reports\n  - Type evaluation report: No. 0511-ER-V053-22 dated 10th August 2022, 381 pages including annexes 1-5\n  - Test report No. 6015-PT-P5009-22, 21 pages including annex 1\n  - Test report No. 8551-PT-E0072-22, 15 pages including annexes 1\n  - Test report No. 6011-PT-SW012-22, 6 pages including annex 1\n  - Test report No. 6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1, 104 pages including annex 1-4\n  - Test report No. 6015-PT-P5001-22, 94 pages including annexes 1–4\n  - Test report No. 6011-PT-SW009-22, 9 pages including annex 1\n  - Test report No. 8551-PT-E0041-21, 29 pages including annexes 1\n  - Documentation file: 0511-UL-V053-22\n  - Certificate history: Revision 0, 26 May 2022, Issuing certificate; Revision 1, 13 September 2022, Adding electronics MV145\n  - Signed by RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body, date 13 September 2022\n\n- Page 3: Measuring system description\n  - Water meters MS2500 / MV110 and MS2500 / MV145\n  - Electromagnetic water meters with electronic indicating device\n  - Sensor MS2500: steel body, flanges, excitation coils, electrodes; liners: PTFE (DN25-DN350, 90°C), Polypropylene (DN25-DN150, 50°C), Rilsan (DN50-DN350, 50°C), Ebonite (DN200-DN350, 50°C)\n  - Converters MV110 or MV145\n  - Power supply modifications: MV110 HV (100-240V AC), MV110 LV (24-36V AC/DC), MV110 LLV (10-48V DC), MV145 HV + lit battery (100-240V AC with 3.7V DC lithium battery backup), MV145 LLV + alk battery (10-48V DC with 3.7V alkaline), MV145 LLV + lit battery (10-48V DC with 3.7V lithium)\n\n- Pages 4-11: Characteristics tables per nominal diameter\n  - DN25, DN32, DN40 (page 4)\n  - DN50, DN65, DN80 (page 6)\n  - DN100, DN125, DN150 (page 7)\n  - DN200, DN250, DN300 (page 9) - MV110 only\n  - DN350 (page 10) - MV110 only\n\nLet me tabulate:\n\nDN25: Q1 = 0.064 (R250), 0.040 (R400); Q2 = 0.102, 0.064; Q3 = 16.00; Q4 = 20.00\nDN32: Q1 = 0.100 (R250), 0.062 (R400); Q2 = 0.160, 0.100; Q3 = 25.00; Q4 = 31.25\nDN40: Q1 = 0.160 (R250), 0.100 (R400); Q2 = 0.256, 0.160; Q3 = 40.00; Q4 = 50.00\nDN50: Q1 = 0.252 (R250), 0.158 (R400); Q2 = 0.403, 0.252; Q3 = 63.0; Q4 = 73.0\nDN65: Q1 = 0.400 (R250), 0.250 (R400); Q2 = 0.640, 0.400; Q3 = 100; Q4 = 125\nDN80: Q1 = 0.640 (R250), 0.400 (R400); Q2 = 1.024, 0.640; Q3 = 160; Q4 = 200\nDN100: Q1 = 1.00 (R250), 0.63 (R400); Q2 = 1.60, 1.00; Q3 = 250; Q4 = 313\nDN125: Q1 = 1.60 (R250), 1.00 (R400); Q2 = 2.56, 1.60; Q3 = 400; Q4 = 500\nDN150: Q1 = 2.52 (R250), 1.60 (R400); Q2 = 4.03, 1.56 (note: probably should be 1.60, but OCR says 1.56); Q3 = 630; Q4 = 780\nDN200: Q1 = 4.0 (R250), 2.5 (R400); Q2 = 6.4, 4.0; Q3 = 1000; Q4 = 1250\nDN250: Q1 = 6.4 (R250), 4.0 (R400); Q2 = 10.2, 6.4; Q3 = 1600; Q4 = 2000\nDN300: Q1 = 6.4 (R250), 4.0 (R400); Q2 = 10.2, 6.4; Q3 = 1600; Q4 = 2000\nDN350 (MV110 only): Q1 = 6.4 (R250), 4.0 (R400); Q2 = 10.2, 6.4; Q3 = 1600; Q4 = 2000\n\nQ2/Q1 = 1.6 for all\nQ4/Q3 = 1.25 for all\n\nCommon characteristics:\n- Measuring principle: Electromagnetic water meter\n- Accuracy class: 1 and 2 (per R variant)\n- MPEl: ±3% (Class 1), ±5% (Class 2)\n- MPEu: ±1 (class 1) ±2 (class 2) % for water ≤ 30°C; ±2 (class1) ±3 (class 2) % for water > 30°C\n- Temperature class: varies by liner\n- Water pressure class: MAP16\n- Pressure loss class: Δp40\n- Reverse flow: designed to measure\n- Environmental class: O (-25 to +55 °C)\n- Electromagnetic environment: E1, E2\n- Mechanical class: B, O\n- Maximum admissible temperature: 90°C for PTFE, 50°C for others\n- Maximum admissible pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- Orientation limitation: any\n- Indicating range: 99,999 (DN25); 999,999 (DN32); 999,999 (DN40); 999,999 (DN50); 999,999 (DN65); 999,999 (DN80); 999,999 (DN100-350)... let me re-check.\n\nPage 5 (DN25, 32, 40):\n- Indicating range: 99 999, 99 999, 99 999 — wait, page 4 ends at DN40 table without indicating range. Page 5 continues: Indicating range [m³]: 99 999, 99 999, 99 999. Hmm, but DN40 has Q3=40, Q4=50 m³/h... indicating range 99,999 m³? That seems plausible for an electronic meter. Actually wait, page 5 shows 99 999 for all three (DN25, 32, 40).\n- Resolution of indicating device: 0.000 1, 0.000 1, 0.000 1\n\nPage 6 (DN50, 65, 80):\n- Indicating range: 99 999, 999 999, 999 999\n- Resolution: 0.000 1, 0.001, 0.001\n\nPage 8 (DN100, 125, 150):\n- Indicating range: 999 999, 999 999, 999 999\n- Resolution: 0.001, 0.001, 0.001\n\nPage 9 (DN200, 250, 300):\n- Indicating range: 9 999 999, 9 999 999, 9 999 999\n- Resolution: 0.01, 0.01, 0.01\n\nPage 10-11 (DN350):\n- Indicating range: 9 999 999\n- Resolution: 0.01\n\nLength [mm]:\n- DN25: 200, DN32: 200, DN40: 200 (page 5)\n- DN50: 200, DN65: 200, DN80: 200 (page 7)\n- DN100: 250, DN125: 250, DN150: 300 (page 8)\n- DN200: 350, DN250: 450, DN300: 500 (page 10)\n- DN350: 550 (page 11)\n\nInstallation details (common): Flanged, min straight inlet 0 mm, outlet 0 mm, flow profile sensitivity class U0D0, flow conditioner No, orientation any.\n\nEUT testing: Category Electromagnetic water meter, Case D.\n\nPower supply table (pages 11-12):\n\nMV110:\n| Designation | MS2500/MV110 HV | MS2500/MV110 LV | MS2500/MV110 LLV |\n| Type | 100–240 V AC | 24–36V AC/DC | 10–48 V DC |\n| Frequency | 45–66 Hz | 45–66 Hz | - |\n| Internal backup source | 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | 3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | - |\n| Min battery life | - | - | - |\n| SW version | V.1.04.0005.0004 | | |\n| CRC | 9BA34F3B | | |\n| Cut-off | 0.2% | | |\n| Detection empty pipe | on | | |\n\nMV145:\n| Designation | MS2500/MV145 HV + lit. battery | MS2500/MV145 LLV + alk. battery | MS2500/MV145 LLV + lit. battery |\n| Type | 100–240 V AC | 10–48 V DC | 10–48 V DC |\n| Frequency | 45–66 Hz | (empty) | - |\n| Internal backup source | 3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | 3.7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery | - |\n| Min battery life | - | - | - |\n| SW version | V.1.04.0005.0004 for MV110; V.1.05.0004.0005 for MV145 |\n| CRC | 9BA34F3B for MV110; C96453B1 for MV145 |\n| Cut-off | 0.2% |\n| Detection empty pipe | on |\n\nNote: The MV145 HV + lit battery description in page 3 says \"power supply by main 100 – 240V AC with internal backup source 3.7V DC lithium battery\" — consistent.\n\nWait, on page 12 the first table row starts with \"Internal backup source\" — this is continuation of the MV110 table from page 11. So the MV110 table: HV and LV have backup source 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery; LLV has \"-\".\n\nHmm interesting — page 3 says MV110 HV has mains 100-240V AC... but the power supply table says HV has internal backup 3.7V battery. OK.\n\nNow, model family. The cert covers:\n- Two electronics: MV110 and MV145\n- Sensor MS2500\n- 13 nominal diameters: DN25, 32, 40, 50, 65, 80, 100, 125, 150, 200, 250, 300, 350\n- Two R ratios: R250 (Q3/Q1=250) and R400 (Q3/Q1=400)\n- Module variants: MS2500/MV110 HV, LV, LLV; MS2500/MV145 HV + lit, LLV + alk, LLV + lit\n\nHow to model this? The schema asks for model_family with models[] entries. The natural model axis could be combination of DN × R ratio × power variant. That would be 13 × 2 × (3 or 3) = 78 variants. That's too many to enumerate. Better approach: use config_level for the DN axis and R axis.\n\nActually the model_level could be keyed by DN. The characteristics matrix: attribute × model. If we use model_id = \"DN25\", \"DN32\" etc., we can have model_level entries for q1, q2, q3, q4. But q1 and q2 differ per R ratio too. So q1 has two values per DN (R250 and R400). Could use config_level with axis \"flow_ratio_r\" (Q3/Q1): values {condition: \"R250\", value}, {condition: \"R400\", value} per DN... Hmm.\n\nLet me think about the cleanest representation:\n\n- model_family.models: one entry per nominal diameter? Or per module designation (6 variants)? The \"certified type\" is MS2500/MV110 and MS2500/MV145. The models are the 6 module designations. But the flow characteristics vary by DN.\n\nAlternative: models keyed by module designation (6 models), and DN × R as config axes with config_level entries.\n\nActually let me reconsider. The DN axis is the primary varying axis in the big tables. Each DN has two R-ratio variants (class 1 and class 2 correspond to R250 and R400? Let me check: \"Accuracy class: 1 | 2 | 1 | 2...\" per column. Q3/Q1: 250 | 400 | 250 | 400...). \n\nHmm, in OIML R49 water meters, accuracy class 1 and 2 are the classes. The Q3/Q1 ratio of 250 vs 400 — actually looking at the column alignment: for DN25, Q1 values are 0.064 and 0.040, with Q3/Q1 = 250 and 400. And accuracy class row shows \"1 | 2\". So the first column variant (Q1=0.064, R250) is class 1, and second (Q1=0.040, R400) is class 2. Interesting — that means class 1 meters have R250 and class 2 have R400? That doesn't seem like a rule, but per the table alignment, each DN has two variants: class 1 with Q3/Q1=250, class 2 with Q3/Q1=400.\n\nSo the models per DN: (class 1, R250) and (class 2, R400).\n\nGiven complexity, I'll model:\n- model_family.models: 6 entries for the module designations (MS2500/MV110 HV, LV, LLV; MS2500/MV145 HV+lit, LLV+alk, LLV+lit)? Or 13 DN entries?\n\nHmm. The schema says \"model_family: include ONLY if cert covers >1 model variant\". The variants here are best captured as nominal diameters since the flow characteristics tables are organized by DN. The power supply variants are configuration axes (config_level).\n\nI think a good approach:\n- model_family.family_name: \"MS2500\" (sensor family) or null. Actually the type designations are MS2500/MV110 and MS2500/MV145. Let me use models keyed by DN: \"DN25\", \"DN32\", ..., \"DN350\" (13 models).\n- model_level characteristics: q1 (needs 2 values per DN — use config_level instead), q2, q3, q4, indicating_range, resolution_of_indicating_device, length.\n\nFor q1/q2 which vary by DN and by R ratio: use config_level with axis \"accuracy_class_and_ratio\"? Hmm, config_level values: {condition, value}. Condition could be \"DN25 / R250 (class 1)\". \n\nAlternatively, keep it simpler:\n- model_level: attribute q3, q4 per DN model.\n- config_level: axis \"meter_ratio_q3_over_q1\" with conditions... but config_level doesn't have model dimension. The schema: config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. So one config_level entry per attribute, with conditions like \"DN25, R250\" → value. That works.\n\nLet me define:\n- config_level entries for q1 and q2: axis \"nominal_diameter_and_ratio\" with conditions \"DN25 R250\", \"DN25 R400\", etc. 26 values each. That's a lot but accurate.\n\nAlternatively, model_level for q1 with values per model where model = \"DN25 R250\" etc. — but then q3 etc. would also need 26 entries. Hmm.\n\nActually, maybe simpler: models[] = 26 entries \"DN25 R250 (class 1)\", \"DN25 R400 (class 2)\", etc. Then model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, accuracy_class... q3, q4 same for both variants of a DN, so repeated. That's verbose but machine-friendly.\n\nLet me weigh: The R137 example uses models per size (G1.6, G2.5) and model_level with one value per model. Here we have a genuine 2D structure: DN × R ratio. The R49 R400 values: Q1 = Q3/400, Q2 = 1.6×Q1.\n\nI think cleanest: \n- models: 13 DN entries (model_id \"DN25\" ... \"DN350\").\n- model_level: q3, q4, indicating_range, indicating_device_resolution, length (one value per DN).\n- config_level: \n  - attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"accuracy_class_flow_ratio\", values: [{condition: \"class 1, Q3/Q1=250\", value...}] — but wait, we need per-DN values too. config_level condition can encode both: \"DN25, class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\".\n\nHmm, that's 26 conditions. OK, I'll do that. Similarly q2.\n\nAlternatively, since Q1 = Q3/R and Q2 = 1.6*Q1, I could store q1 as formula strings. But rules say don't fabricate; giving actual values is better.\n\nAlso I should include matrix_tables[] to preserve the per-DN tables. I can create one matrix table \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter\" with columns dn, q1_class1_r250, q1_class2_r400, q2_class1, q2_class2, q3, q4, length, indicating_range, resolution. That preserves everything neatly.\n\nLet me build the rows:\n\n| DN | q1_r250 | q1_r400 | q2_r250 | q2_r400 | q3 | q4 | length_mm | indicating_range_m3 | resolution_m3 |\n| 25 | 0.064 | 0.040 | 0.102 | 0.064 | 16 | 20 | 200 | 99999 | 0.0001 |\n| 32 | 0.100 | 0.062 | 0.160 | 0.100 | 25 | 31.25 | 200 | 99999 | 0.0001 |\n| 40 | 0.160 | 0.100 | 0.256 | 0.160 | 40 | 50 | 200 | 99999 | 0.0001 |\n| 50 | 0.252 | 0.158 | 0.403 | 0.252 | 63 | 73 | 200 | 99999 | 0.0001 |\n| 65 | 0.400 | 0.250 | 0.640 | 0.400 | 100 | 125 | 200 | 999999 | 0.001 |\n| 80 | 0.640 | 0.400 | 1.024 | 0.640 | 160 | 200 | 200 | 999999 | 0.001 |\n| 100 | 1.00 | 0.63 | 1.60 | 1.00 | 250 | 313 | 250 | 999999 | 0.001 |\n| 125 | 1.60 | 1.00 | 2.56 | 1.60 | 400 | 500 | 250 | 999999 | 0.001 |\n| 150 | 2.52 | 1.60 | 4.03 | 1.56 | 630 | 780 | 300 | 999999 | 0.001 |\n| 200 | 4.0 | 2.5 | 6.4 | 4.0 | 1000 | 1250 | 350 | 9999999 | 0.01 |\n| 250 | 6.4 | 4.0 | 10.2 | 6.4 | 1600 | 2000 | 450 | 9999999 | 0.01 |\n| 300 | 6.4 | 4.0 | 10.2 | 6.4 | 1600 | 2000 | 500 | 9999999 | 0.01 |\n| 350 | 6.4 | 4.0 | 10.2 | 6.4 | 1600 | 2000 | 550 | 9999999 | 0.01 |\n\nNote DN150 q2 for R400 is given as 1.56 in the OCR (likely a typo for 1.60 but I shouldn't fabricate/correct... Actually rule 8 says don't fabricate. The OCR says 1.56. Hmm, 1.6*1.60 = 2.56... no wait: Q1=1.60 for R400, Q2 = 1.6 × 1.60 = 2.56. But table says 1.56. Let me re-check page 7: DN150 row Q2: \"4.03\" (R250) and \"1.56\" (R400). Wait no: DN125: Q2 = 2.56, 1.60. DN150: Q2 = 4.03, 1.56. For DN150 R250: Q1=2.52, Q2 = 1.6×2.52 = 4.032 ≈ 4.03 ✓. For R400: Q1=1.60, Q2 = 1.6×1.60 = 2.56, but table says 1.56. Hmm, that's inconsistent — likely OCR error (1.56 vs 2.56). But I must not fabricate. I'll record 1.56 as printed? Hmm. Actually with OCR outputs, \"2.56\" could easily be misread as \"1.56\"? Not typically, but possible. The instruction: don't fabricate. I'll record what's in the markdown: 1.56. Actually wait, let me re-read: \"<td>1.56</td>\" — yes it says 1.56. Hmm, but also DN250 Q2 for R400 is 6.4 with Q1=4.0 → 6.4 ✓ (1.6×4.0=6.4). DN200: Q1=2.5, Q2=4.0 ✓. DN150 R400: Q1=1.60, Q2 should be 2.56. Table says 1.56 which is likely a certificate typo anyway. I'll keep 1.56 as-is.\n\nHmm wait, actually, for DN150 the R400 Q1 is 1.60 and Q2 1.56?? That would make Q2 < Q1 which is nonsense. It's almost certainly a typo in the cert for 2.56. But per rule \"don't fabricate\", I'll record 1.56. Or should I? The rule is about not inventing data. Recording the printed value is faithful. I'll record 1.56.\n\nNow DN350 note: \"MS2500 / MV110\" only — page 10 table says Model number: MS2500 / MV110, Nominal diameter: 350. Also DN200-300: Model number: MS2500 / MV110 (page 9). Interesting! So MV145 is only for DN25-DN150. Good detail: pages 9-10 say \"Model number: MS2500 / MV110\" for DN200, 250, 300, 350.\n\nNow the module/power variants. Power supply table gives 6 module designations. Each with:\n- supply type/voltage: HV: 100–240 V AC; LV: 24–36 V AC/DC; LLV: 10–48 V DC; MV145 HV: 100–240 V AC; MV145 LLV+alk: 10–48 V DC; MV145 LLV+lit: 10–48 V DC\n- frequency: 45–66 Hz for HV and LV variants; \"-\" for DC ones. MV145 LLV+alk: empty (blank).\n- internal backup: HV (MV110): 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery; LV (MV110): 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery; LLV: -; MV145 HV+lit: 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery; MV145 LLV+alk: 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery; MV145 LLV+lit: -.\n\nHmm wait — page 3 text says MV110 HV has mains 100-240V AC (no mention of battery), but the table shows internal backup 3.7V for MV110 HV and LV. OK, follow the table.\n\nSoftware versions: MV110 table: V.1.04.0005.0004; CRC 9BA34F3B. MV145 table: V.1.04.0005.0004 for MV110; V.1.05.0004.0005 for MV145; CRC 9BA34F3B for MV110, C96453B1 for MV145. So MV145: SW V.1.05.0004.0005, CRC C96453B1.\n\nCut-off: 0.2%. Detection empty pipe: on.\n\nComponents:\n- Measuring sensor: MS2500\n- Converters (calculating/indicating device): MV110, MV145\n\nComponents array:\n1. role: \"measuring sensor\", type_designations: [\"MS2500\"], characteristics: liner options (PTFE DN25-DN350 up to 90°C, Polypropylene DN25-DN150 50°C, Rilsan DN50-DN350 50°C, Ebonite DN200-DN350 50°C).\n2. role: \"converter / electronic indicating device\", type_designations: [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"], characteristics: LCD display, impulse output, current output, RS485, USB mini-B.\n\nCharacteristics type_level:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- environmental_class: \"O\" with temperature range -25 to +55 °C. I could emit as environmental_class: {value: \"O\"} plus environmental_class_temperature_range: {min:-25,max:55, unit °C}.\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\" → value \"E1/E2\"? Keep as string \"E1, E2\".\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6, unit \"MPa\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\" — value \"Δp40\" string.\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- accuracy classes: [1, 2] (in recommendation.accuracy_classes)\n- mpe_lower_zone: value string \"±3% (class 1), ±5% (class 2)\" — hmm. Could structure as config_level with axis accuracy_class. Let me do config_level entries:\n  - attribute \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\", axis \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition \"class 1\", value 3, unit \"%\", sign}, ...]. Values are ±3% and ±5%. I'll put value as string \"±3\" with unit \"%\"? Or value 3? The ± sign matters. I'll use value: \"±3%\" as string? Rule: structured values... percent ±3%. Hmm, value could be -3/+3? I'll use string \"±3\" with unit \"%\" — but rule says numbers not strings where possible. The ± prefix though... I could use {\"value\": \"±3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}. That seems acceptable since it's a tolerance expression. Alternatively record as type_level strings:\n  - mpe_lower_zone: {\"value\": \"±3% (class 1); ±5% (class 2)\"} — simpler but less structured.\n  \n  I'll use config_level with two entries (lower/upper × conditions). Actually let me do:\n  - config_level: {attribute: \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: \"±3\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2\", value: \"±5\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}]}\n  - config_level: {attribute: \"maximum_permissible_error_upper_zone\", axis: \"accuracy_class_and_water_temperature\", values: [{condition: \"class 1, water ≤ 30 °C\", value: \"±1\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2, water ≤ 30 °C\", value: \"±2\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 1, water > 30 °C\", value: \"±2\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"class 2, water > 30 °C\", value: \"±3\", unit_symbol: \"%\"}]}\n\n  That's clean.\n\n- temperature classes by liner — components (sensor) characteristics:\n  - PTFE liner: DN25-DN350, water temp up to 90°C, temperature classes T90, T50, T30\n  - Polypropylene: DN25-DN150, 50°C, T50, T30\n  - Rilsan: DN50-DN350, 50°C, T50 T30 from DN50\n  - Ebonite: DN200-DN350, 50°C, T50 T30 from DN200\n\n- maximum_admissible_temperature: 90 °C (PTFE liner); 50 °C (other liners) — config_level axis \"liner\".\n\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet: 0 mm; outlet: 0 mm.\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- eut_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"; case: \"D\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: U0D0 (does not differ from U0 D0)\n- cut_off: 0.2, unit \"%\"\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\"\n- volume_resolution: 0.001 m³ displayed (page 3 says \"displays the volume resolution of 0.001 m3\") — but the table gives per-DN resolution. Use per-DN.\n\nConfig level for liner temps:\n- attribute \"maximum_admissible_temperature\", axis \"liner_material\", values: [{condition: \"PTFE\", value: 90, unit °C}, {condition: \"Polypropylene\", value: 50}, {condition: \"Rilsan\", value: 50}, {condition: \"Ebonite\", value: 50}]\n\nPower supply variants — this is a configuration axis \"power_supply_variant\" (6 conditions) with attributes: supply_voltage, supply_type, frequency, internal_backup_source. Let me do config_level:\n\n- attribute \"power_supply_voltage\", axis \"module_variant\", values:\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 HV\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LV\", value: {min:24,max:36}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV110 LLV\", value: {min:10,max:48}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV145 HV + lithium battery\", value: {min:100,max:240}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV145 LLV + alkaline battery\", value: {min:10,max:48}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n  - {condition: \"MS2500/MV145 LLV + lithium battery\", value: {min:10,max:48}, unit_symbol: \"V\"}\n- attribute \"power_supply_type\", axis \"module_variant\": AC, AC/DC, DC, AC, DC, DC\n- attribute \"power_supply_frequency\", axis \"module_variant\": 45–66 Hz, 45–66 Hz, null(DC), 45–66 Hz, null(blank), null(-)\n  For the DC ones the table has \"-\" meaning not applicable. I'll record value: null? Rule: empty strings are not values, use null or omit. For conditions with \"-\", I can either omit those conditions or value null. I'll include with value: null to preserve the matrix? Hmm — \"don't fabricate... Empty strings are not values — use null or omit.\" I'll include them as null to keep 6-column alignment. Actually cleaner: include only non-null. I'll include with null for clarity.\n\n- attribute \"internal_backup_source\", axis \"module_variant\": \n  - MV110 HV: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\" — structured: voltage 3.7 V DC, capacity 5200 mAh, type lithium battery. I'll keep as string \"3.7 V DC, 5200 mAh battery\" — or two attributes: backup_source_voltage 3.7 V; backup_source_capacity 5200 mAh. The table cell says \"3,7V DC; 5200mAh; Battery\". Page 3 says lithium for HV+lit, alka for alk. Keep string value to be safe: {\"value\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\"}. Hmm, mixed structure. I think strings fine here.\n\n- software versions and CRC: type_level-ish per electronics:\n  - software_version: MV110 → \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"; MV145 → \"V.1.05.0004.0005\" — config_level axis \"electronics\": values [{condition \"MV110\", value \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"}, {condition \"MV145\", value \"V.1.05.0004.0005\"}]\n  - crc_checksum: MV110 \"9BA34F3B\"; MV145 \"C96453B1\"\n\nModel-level characteristics (per DN):\n- q3 (transitional flowrate, m³/h), q4 (maximum flowrate), q1 (minimum flowrate) — but q1/q2 vary by ratio. Use config_level with axis combining DN & ratio. Or model_level with model_id \"DN25 R250\". Hmm.\n\nDecision: I'll make model_level for attributes that have a single value per DN: q3, q4, indicating_range, indicating_device_resolution, sensor_length. And config_level (axis \"nominal_diameter_and_accuracy_class_ratio\") for q1 and q2 with 26 entries each. Alternatively make model_level with model ids \"DN25/class1 (R250)\"... \n\nActually, maybe better: models[] = 26 variants, model_id like \"DN25-R250\" and \"DN25-R400\", with attributes in model_level for q1, q2, q3, q4, etc. That's the most machine-queryable. But it's huge: 26 models × attributes. The JSON will be long but that's fine. Hmm, but indicating_range/resolution/length are per DN (same for both R variants) — repeated 2× each in model_level. That's ok.\n\nAlternatively hybrid: models keyed by DN (13), model_level for q3, q4, indicating_range, resolution, length; config_level for q1, q2 with conditions \"DN25 class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\". I think hybrid is cleaner and follows the R137 example pattern (models per size). The config axis is then the \"ratio/class\" dimension.\n\nI'll go hybrid:\n\nmodels: 13 entries: {\"model_id\": \"DN25\"}, ..., {\"model_id\": \"DN350\"} with attributes maybe {\"accuracy_classes\": ...}? Keep simple: model_id only, plus label maybe. The schema: models[]: model_id, label, attributes{}. I can add attributes: {\"accuracy_class\": [1,2]}. Eh, keep model_id only.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. q3 (transitional_flowrate_q3): values per DN: 16, 25, 40, 63, 100, 160, 250, 400, 630, 1000, 1600, 1600, 1600 (m³/h). Wait DN50: 63.0; DN65: 100; DN80: 160; DN100: 250; DN125: 400; DN150: 630; DN200: 1000; DN250: 1600; DN300: 1600; DN350: 1600.\n2. q4 (maximum_flowrate_q4): 20, 31.25, 50, 73, 125, 200, 313, 500, 780, 1250, 2000, 2000, 2000.\n3. indicating_range: 99999, 99999, 99999, 99999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 9999999, 9999999, 9999999, 9999999 (m³).\n4. resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01 (m³).\n5. length: 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 300, 350, 450, 500, 550 (mm).\n\nconfig_level:\n- attribute \"minimum_flowrate_q1\", axis \"accuracy_class_ratio\", values: 26 entries {condition: \"DN25, class 1, Q3/Q1=250\", value: 0.064, unit: m³/h} etc.\n- attribute \"transitional_flowrate_q2\" (actually Q2 is the transitional flow): 26 entries.\n- attribute \"flow_ratio_q3_over_q1\", axis ... hmm this is derivable; maybe include as config: conditions \"class 1\" → 250, \"class 2\" → 400 across all DNs. Type-level? The ratio varies by accuracy class config: config_level {attribute: \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", axis: \"accuracy_class\", values: [{condition: \"class 1\", value: 250}, {condition: \"class 2\", value: 400}]}. Nice.\n- q2_over_q1 = 1.6 (type_level dimensionless).\n- q4_over_q3 = 1.25 (type_level).\n- MPE entries as above.\n- max admissible temp by liner.\n- power supply variants by module_variant axis.\n- software version & CRC by electronics axis.\n\nmatrix_tables: I'll include one comprehensive table:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter\",\n  \"columns\": [\"nominal_diameter\", \"accuracy_class\", \"ratio_q3_over_q1\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\"],\n  ... \n}\n\nOr rows per DN with both class variants. Let me structure rows as objects:\n\n{\"nominal_diameter\": \"DN25\", \"q1_class1_r250\": 0.064, \"q2_class1_r250\": 0.102, \"q1_class2_r400\": 0.040, \"q2_class2_r400\": 0.064, \"q3\": 16, \"q4\": 20, \"length_mm\": 200, \"indicating_range_m3\": 99999, \"resolution_m3\": 0.0001}\n\nUnits: column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\"].\n\nThe schema for matrix_tables: name, columns[], rows[] (object per row), description, footnotes[]. The R137 example adds \"column_units\". I'll include column_units.\n\nAlso a second matrix table for power supply:\n\n{\n  \"name\": \"Power supply and electrical specification\",\n  \"columns\": [\"module_designation\", \"electronics\", \"supply\", \"frequency\", \"internal_backup_source\", \"minimum_battery_life_years\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"cut_off\", \"empty_pipe_detection\"],\n  \"rows\": [\n    {\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"electronics\": \"MV110\", \"supply\": \"100–240 V AC\", \"frequency\": \"45–66 Hz\", \"internal_backup_source\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\", ...},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, rows with mixed strings fine.\n\ncertificate object:\n- number: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" — hmm canonical? The rule for recommendation.id says canonical e.g. \"R60\". For certificate.number, keep as printed: \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\". Add revision? The schema has revision_history separately. Maybe include \"revision\": but schema doesn't have it at certificate level. There's revision_history. The current revision is 1. I could note in revision_history. Fine.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- project_number: not present — omit. Hmm, \"CZ1\" is the authority number. Not a project number. Omit.\n- page_total: 15\n- member_state: \"Czech Republic\"\n- date_issued: \"2022-09-13\"\n- oiml_issuer_id: not explicitly given. Hmm — CMI is the issuing authority; oiml_issuer_id... not in the doc. Omit? The certificate number contains \"CZ1\" which is the authority identifier. I'll omit oiml_issuer_id since not explicitly present. Actually, hmm. CMI's OIML issuer ID... not stated. Omit.\n\nissuing_authority:\n- name: \"Czech Metrology Institute\"\n- address_lines: [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"]\n- person_responsible: \"Jan Klandra\" — wait \"Jan Kalandra\". \n- person_title: not given for Kalandra; the signer is \"RNDr. Pavel Klenovský, Head of Certification Body\". The schema has person_responsible and person_title. person_title could be null/omit; but maybe I should include signer? The schema fields: person_responsible, person_title. I'll set person_responsible: \"Jan Kalandra\", person_title: null → omit. Hmm, maybe add to notes? There's no notes field. I could put person_title as \"Head of Certification Body\" but that belongs to Klenovský, not Kalandra. To be faithful, omit person_title. \n\napplicants: [{name: \"ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"]}]\nmanufacturers: [{name: \"Hemina SPA\", address_lines: [\"Via Piemonte 1\", \"Montagnana – PD\", \"350 44 Italy\"]}]. Page 4 table says \"Via Piemonte 2; Montagnana; 350 44 Italy\". Page 1 says \"Via Piemonte 1\". I'll use page 1 address but note... The discrepancy: page 1 \"Via Piemonte 1, Montagnana – PD; PC: 350 44, Italy\". Keep that. (Page 15 label says Via Piemonte, 2. Whatever — use page 1.)\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\" — the schema examples list \"Gas meter\" etc. This is a water meter (electromagnetic). Use \"Water meter\".\n- type_designations: [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\"]. Also MS2500 sensor, MV110, MV145 converters. The certified types as identified: MS2500 / MV110 and MS2500 / MV145. Include components separately. I'll put type_designations: [\"MS2500/MV110\", \"MS2500/MV145\"]. Hmm, keep spaces as printed: \"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\".\n- module_designation: \"Not applicable\"? No — modules listed: the six power variants. The schema module_designation is a single string (\"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null). Here multiple modules. I could set module_designation null and capture modules in components or model_family. Hmm. The \"Designation of the module\" section lists 6 combos. I'll set module_designation: null (since it's multi) — or provide the list as string? Schema expects single value. I'll omit it (null) and rely on components + config_level. Actually maybe I can set it to \"MS2500 / MV110 HV; MS2500 / MV110 LV; MS2500 / MV110 LLV; MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\"? That's ugly. Better: components[] entry \"electronic converter module\" with the variants. And module_designation: null... The schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. I'll use null? Hmm, providing the combined string seems informative. I'll go with components instead and set module_designation to null. Hmm, but rule 8: omit if not present. The module designations ARE present though. Let me set:\n\n\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 HV; MS2500 / MV110 LV; MS2500 / MV110 LLV; MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\"\n\nEh. I think it's acceptable to leave null and capture in components. But information loss risk. I'll include the string — it's faithful. Actually let me reconsider: schema type says string with examples. Multi-value string joined by \"; \" is fine.\n\n- description: from page 3: \"The water meters types MS2500 / MV110 and MS2500 / MV145 are electromagnetic water meters with an electronic indicating device, designed to measure, memorise and display the volume at metering conditions of water passing through the measurement transducer. Consist of sensor MS2500 and converter MV110 or MV145 in compact set up or separate version with connecting cable.\"\n\nmodel_family:\n- family_name: \"MS2500\" (sensor family)? Or null. The models are per DN. I'll set family_name: \"MS2500\" — hmm, but model includes converter too. family_name null might be safest, but the sensor MS2500 is common. I'll use \"MS2500\". Hmm... Actually models are the DN sizes of the MS2500 sensor paired with either converter. family_name \"MS2500\" is reasonable. Let me keep null? The rule: \"explicit family name if any, else null\". No explicit family name in doc. → null. But \"Model number: MS2500 / MV110; MS2500 / MV145\" — that's model number, not family. I'll set null.\n\n- models: 13 DN entries. Should I add attributes like accuracy classes? Keep: {\"model_id\": \"DN25\", \"attributes\": {\"accuracy_class\": \"1 and 2\"}}? Overkill. Just model_id. Maybe label: \"DN25 (Q3 = 16 m³/h)\". I'll keep model_id only... Adding label with q3 is nice but redundant. Keep simple: model_id.\n\nWait — should models instead be the 6 module variants? The power variants are configurations rather than separate models. DN is the size axis. I'm going with DN.\n\ncomponents:\n1. {role: \"measuring sensor (measurement transducer)\", type_designations: [\"MS2500\"], alternatives: null, characteristics: {liners: ...}} — characteristics within components: the schema says components[]: role, type_designations[], alternatives, characteristics{}. characteristics presumably an object. I'll include:\n   - liner options as a nested object: {\"liner_materials\": {\"PTFE\": {\"diameter_range\": \"DN25-DN350\", \"max_water_temperature\": 90}, ...}}? That's creative but schema says characteristics{} free-form. Simpler: characteristics: {\"liner_options\": [{\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"diameters\": \"DN25-DN350\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}}, ...]}. I'll do a list of liner option objects.\n\n2. {role: \"converter / electronic indicating device (calculating device)\", type_designations: [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"], characteristics: {\"display\": \"LCD showing volume and flow\", \"outputs\": [\"impulse output\", \"current output\"], \"communication_interfaces\": [\"RS485\", \"USB mini-B\"], \"buttons\": 3, \"controls\": \"three buttons\"}}. Keep modest.\n\nAlso module variants could be a component? The converters MV110/MV145 with power variants covered in config_level. Fine.\n\ncharacteristics:\n- type_level:\n  - measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n  - accuracy_classes: [1, 2] — hmm, characteristics.type_level expects {value, unit_symbol,...}. I could do accuracy_class: {\"value\": \"1 and 2\"}. But recommendation.accuracy_classes holds [1,2]. In type_level maybe \"accuracy_class\": {\"value\": [1,2]}? Value can be number|string|{min,max} per spec... a list isn't specified. I'll skip in type_level (it's in recommendation) or use string \"1, 2\". I'll include {\"value\": \"1, 2\"} for completeness. Hmm, value spec says number | {min,max} | string. String \"1, 2\" fine.\n  - reverse_flow: {\"value\": \"designed to measure\"}\n  - environmental_class: {\"value\": \"O\"}\n  - environmental_class_temperature_range: {\"value\": {min:-25, max:55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n  - electromagnetic_environment: {\"value\": \"E1, E2\"}\n  - mechanical_class: {\"value\": \"B, O\"}\n  - maximum_admissible_pressure: {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"MPa\"}\n  - water_pressure_class: {\"value\": \"MAP16\"}\n  - pressure_loss_class: {\"value\": \"Δp40\"}\n  - flow_profile_sensitivity_class: {\"value\": \"U0D0\"}\n  - installation_sensitivity_class: same U0D0. Both appear (\"The installation sensitivity class (Ux Dx) – it does not differ from U0 D0\" and \"Flow profile sensitivity class: U0D0\"). Include one: installation_sensitivity_class.\n  - connection_type: {\"value\": \"Flanged\"}\n  - minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}\n  - minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}\n  - flow_conditioner: {\"value\": \"No\"}\n  - orientation: {\"value\": \"any\"}\n  - eut_testing_category: {\"value\": \"Electromagnetic water meter\"}\n  - eut_testing_case: {\"value\": \"Case D\"}\n  - ratio_q2_over_q1: {\"value\": 1.6}\n  - ratio_q4_over_q3: {\"value\": 1.25}\n  - cut_off: {\"value\": 0.2, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  - empty_pipe_detection: {\"value\": \"on\"}\n  - internal_backup_source_voltage: 3.7 V DC where applicable... config instead.\n\n- config_level:\n  1. mpe lower zone (axis accuracy_class): conditions class 1 → ±3 %, class 2 → ±5 %.\n  2. mpe upper zone (axis accuracy_class & water temperature): 4 conditions.\n  3. ratio q3/q1 (axis accuracy_class): class 1 → 250, class 2 → 400. Wait — is that right? The table columns pair accuracy class 1 with Q3/Q1=250 and class 2 with 400 for every DN. Yes, per table alignment. I'll note it.\n  4. q1 (axis \"nominal_diameter_and_ratio\"): 26 conditions.\n  5. q2 (same axis): 26 conditions.\n  6. maximum_admissible_temperature (axis liner_material): PTFE 90, others 50.\n  7. temperature_class (axis liner_material): PTFE → \"T90, T50, T30\"; Polypropylene → \"T50, T30\"; Rilsan → \"T50, T30 (from DN50)\"; Ebonite → \"T50, T30 (from DN200)\". Values strings.\n  8. power_supply_voltage (axis module_variant): 6 conditions.\n  9. power_supply_type (axis module_variant): 6.\n  10. power_supply_frequency (axis module_variant): HV 45–66 Hz, LV 45–66 Hz, LLV null; MV145 HV 45–66 Hz, MV145 LLV+alk null, MV145 LLV+lit null.\n  11. internal_backup_source (axis module_variant): strings.\n  12. software_version (axis electronics): MV110, MV145.\n  13. crc_checksum (axis electronics): MV110 9BA34F3B, MV145 C96453B1.\n\nFor values in config_level: same structured form {condition, value, unit_symbol}? The schema: config_level[]: attribute, axis, values[]: {condition, value}. Rule 5 says values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. OK.\n\nFor q1 conditions, include DN and class: \"DN25, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\". \n\nmatrix_tables: \n1. \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter\" — with columns: nominal_diameter, q1_r250_class1, q2_r250_class1, q1_r400_class2, q2_r400_class2, q3, q4, length, indicating_range, indicating_resolution. column_units: [null, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, m³/h, mm, m³, m³].\n\n2. \"Power supply and electrical specification\" — rows for 6 module variants.\n\nMaybe also a \"Indicating range and resolution by nominal diameter\" — no, merged into table 1.\n\ntest_reports:\n1. {id: \"0511-ER-V053-22\", date: \"2022-08-10\", pages: 381, role: \"type_evaluation_report\"} — \"that includes 381 pages including annexes 1-5\". pages: 381.\n2. {id: \"6015-PT-P5009-22\", pages: 21, role: \"test_report\"}\n3. {id: \"8551-PT-E0072-22\", pages: 15, role: \"test_report\"}\n4. {id: \"6011-PT-SW012-22\", pages: 6, role: \"test_report\"}\n5. {id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22\", pages: 104, role: \"test_report\", revision: \"1\"} — schema has no revision field... \"id, date, pages, role\". I could encode revision in id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Rev. 1\"? The doc lists both \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1 (104 pages)\" and \"6015-PT-P5001-22 (94 pages)\" as separate entries. I'll keep ids as printed and maybe add a \"revision\" extra field — extra fields are usually tolerated. I'll add \"revision\": \"1\" for one. Schema says fields id, date, pages, role — I'll stick strictly to those and encode as \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1\". Hmm. Strictly: use id \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1\"? That pollutes the id. I'll add revision field anyway; minor deviation acceptable? Risky for validators. I'll encode in id string: \"6015-PT-P5001-22 Rev. 1\". Actually, let me just add \"revision\": \"1\" — the layered schema elsewhere tolerates extras? Unknown. I'll keep id as \"6015-PT-P5001-22\" with pages 104 and add revision \"1\". Hmm...\n\nLet me just include both reports with ids as printed (\"6015-PT-P5001-22 Revision 1\" and \"6015-PT-P5001-22\") — faithful to doc. Fine.\n\n6. {id: \"6015-PT-P5001-22\", pages: 94}\n7. {id: \"6011-PT-SW009-22\", pages: 9}\n8. {id: \"8551-PT-E0041-21\", pages: 29}\n\ndocumentation file: 0511-UL-V053-22 → test_reports entry with role \"documentation_file\", no pages. {id: \"0511-UL-V053-22\", role: \"documentation_file\"}.\n\nrevision_history:\n- {revision: \"0\", date: \"2022-05-26\", changes: \"Issuing certificate\"}\n- {revision: \"1\", date: \"2022-09-13\", changes: \"Adding electronics MV145\"}\n\nfootnotes: none in this doc. Empty or omit. Omit.\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2013 — \"Edition (year): 2013\". edition is int. 2013.\n- amendment: none — omit.\n- scheme: \"A\"\n- accuracy_classes: [1, 2]\n\ncertificate.date_issued: The signature date is 13 September 2022. Revision 1 date. Use \"2022-09-13\".\n\nNow, description of sensor liners — components[0].characteristics:\n\n\"liner_options\": [\n  {\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"nominal_diameters\": {\"value\": {min: 25, max: 350}, ...}} hmm diameters as DN list: \"DN25 – DN350\". I'll do string \"DN25–DN350\".\n  ...\n]\n\nActually simpler: characteristics: {\"liners\": [{\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"dn_range\": \"DN25-DN350\", \"max_water_temperature_c\": 90, \"temperature_classes\": [\"T90\",\"T50\",\"T30\"]}, {\"material\": \"Polypropylene\", \"dn_range\": \"DN25-DN150\", \"max_water_temperature_c\": 50, \"temperature_classes\": [\"T50\",\"T30\"]}, {\"material\": \"Rilsan\", \"dn_range\": \"DN50-DN350\", \"max_water_temperature_c\": 50, \"temperature_classes\": [\"T50\",\"T30\"]}, {\"material\": \"Ebonite\", \"dn_range\": \"DN200-DN350\", \"max_water_temperature_c\": 50, \"temperature_classes\": [\"T50\",\"T30\"]}]}\n\nWait — page 4 table says \"For liner PTFE - T90; T50; T30\" — so PTFE supports T30, T50, T90 classes. Polypropylene: T50, T30. Rilsan: T50, T30 from DN50. Ebonite: T50, T30 from DN200. And page 3 liner availability: PTFE all sizes DN25-DN350; Polypropylene DN25–DN150; Rilsan DN50-DN350; Ebonite DN200-DN350. Good.\n\nNow double check q1/q2 values:\n\nPage 4 (DN25, 32, 40):\nQ1: 0.064, 0.040 | 0.100, 0.062 | 0.160, 0.100\nQ2: 0.102, 0.064 | 0.160, 0.100 | 0.256, 0.160\nQ3: 16.00 | 25.00 | 40.00\nQ4: 20.00 | 31.25 | 50.00\nQ3/Q1: 250, 400 | 250, 400 | 250, 400\nAccuracy class: 1, 2 | 1, 2 | 1, 2\n\nPage 6 (DN50, 65, 80):\nQ1: 0.252, 0.158 | 0.400, 0.250 | 0.640, 0.400\nQ2: 0.403, 0.252 | 0.640, 0.400 | 1.024, 0.640\nQ3: 63.0 | 100 | 160\nQ4: 73.0 | 125 | 200\n\nNote DN50: Q4 = 73.0 (Q4/Q3 = 73/63 = 1.159, not 1.25 — table says Q4/Q3 = 1.25; 63×1.25=78.75... but printed 73.0). Whatever — record as printed.\n\nPage 7 (DN100, 125, 150):\nQ1: 1.00, 0.63 | 1.60, 1.00 | 2.52, 1.60\nQ2: 1.60, 1.00 | 2.56, 1.60 | 4.03, 1.56\nQ3: 250 | 400 | 630\nQ4: 313 | 500 | 780\n\nPage 9 (DN200, 250, 300) — MV110 only:\nQ1: 4.0, 2.5 | 6.4, 4.0 | 6.4, 4.0\nQ2: 6.4, 4.0 | 10.2, 6.4 | 10.2, 6.4\nQ3: 1000 | 1600 | 1600\nQ4: 1250 | 2000 | 2000\n\nPage 10 (DN350) — MV110 only:\nQ1: 6.4, 4.0; Q2: 10.2, 6.4; Q3: 1600; Q4: 2000\n\nIndicating range:\nPage 5 (DN25,32,40): 99 999, 99 999, 99 999 → 99999\nPage 6 (DN50,65,80): 99 999, 999 999, 999 999\nPage 8 (DN100,125,150): 999 999 ×3\nPage 9 (DN200,250,300): 9 999 999 ×3\nPage 11 (DN350): 9 999 999\n\nResolution:\nPage 5: 0.000 1 ×3 → 0.0001\nPage 6: 0.000 1, 0.001, 0.001\nPage 8: 0.001 ×3\nPage 9: 0.01 ×3\nPage 11: 0.01\n\nLength:\nPage 5 (DN25,32,40): 200, 200, 200\nPage 7 (DN50,65,80): 200, 200, 200\nPage 8 (DN100,125,150): 250, 250, 300\nPage 10 (DN200,250,300): 350, 450, 500\nPage 11 (DN350): 550\n\nPressure loss class Δp40 all DNs.\n\nMV145 applicability: DN25–DN150 (pages 4,6,7 say \"Model number: MS2500 / MV110; MS2500 / MV145\"). DN200+: MV110 only. Add to model attributes? I can add attribute in model entries: {\"model_id\": \"DN200\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\"]}} and for DN25-150: {\"converters\": [\"MV110\",\"MV145\"]}. That's useful. I'll include.\n\nNow, config_level q1/q2 26 entries each. Conditions: \"DN25, class 1, Q3/Q1 = 250\". Let me write them:\n\nq1 values (R250/class1, R400/class2):\nDN25: 0.064 / 0.040\nDN32: 0.100 / 0.062\nDN40: 0.160 / 0.100\nDN50: 0.252 / 0.158\nDN65: 0.400 / 0.250\nDN80: 0.640 / 0.400\nDN100: 1.00 / 0.63\nDN125: 1.60 / 1.00\nDN150: 2.52 / 1.60\nDN200: 4.0 / 2.5\nDN250: 6.4 / 4.0\nDN300: 6.4 / 4.0\nDN350: 6.4 / 4.0\n\nq2 values:\nDN25: 0.102 / 0.064\nDN32: 0.160 / 0.100\nDN40: 0.256 / 0.160\nDN50: 0.403 / 0.252\nDN65: 0.640 / 0.400\nDN80: 1.024 / 0.640\nDN100: 1.60 / 1.00\nDN125: 2.56 / 1.60\nDN150: 4.03 / 1.56\nDN200: 6.4 / 4.0\nDN250: 10.2 / 6.4\nDN300: 10.2 / 6.4\nDN350: 10.2 / 6.4\n\nUnit: m³/h.\n\nNow the JSON size: manageable.\n\nStructure plan:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\",\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"page_total\": 15,\n    \"member_state\": \"Czech Republic\",\n    \"date_issued\": \"2022-09-13\",\n    \"revision\": \"1\"  // extra? maybe omit. I'll omit to stay strict. Actually revision info is in revision_history. Omit.\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Czech Metrology Institute\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Okružní 31\", \"638 00 Brno\", \"Czech Republic\"],\n    \"person_responsible\": \"Jan Kalandra\",\n    \"person_title\": \"Head of Certification Body\"? — no, that's Klenovský. Hmm. I'll omit person_title. Or add \"signatory\": extra field? Schema fixed. Omit.\n  },\n  \"applicants\": [{\n    \"name\": \"ISOIL INDUSTRIA SPA\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Via F.lli Gracchi, 27\", \"Cinisello Balsamo\", \"20 099 Italy\"]\n  }],\n  \"manufacturers\": [{\n    \"name\": \"Hemina SPA\",\n    \"address_lines\": [\"Via Piemonte 1\", \"Montagnana – PD\", \"350 44 Italy\"]\n  }],\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500 / MV110\", \"MS2500 / MV145\"],\n    \"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 HV; MS2500 / MV110 LV; MS2500 / MV110 LLV; MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery; MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\",\n    \"description\": \"...\"\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [13 entries]\n  },\n  \"components\": [2 entries],\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {...},\n    \"model_level\": [5 entries × 13 values],\n    \"config_level\": [several]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [2 entries],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2013,\n    \"scheme\": \"A\",\n    \"accuracy_classes\": [1, 2]\n  },\n  \"test_reports\": [...],\n  \"revision_history\": [...]\n}\n\nWait — accuracy class per column pairing: class 1 ↔ R250, class 2 ↔ R400. Is that a coincidence of the table? In R49 water meters, both class 1 and class 2 meters exist; the R ratio (Q3/Q1) is a separate parameter. Here the manufacturer chose to certify class 1 meters with R250 and class 2 with R400. Actually more likely: for class 1, minimum R250 allowed; for class 2 R400... In any case the table pairs them, so I'll represent it.\n\nType-level MPE: since class varies, use config_level.\n\nDescription string: \"Electromagnetic water meter with electronic indicating device, designed to measure, memorise and display the volume of water passing through the measurement transducer at metering conditions. Consists of sensor MS2500 and converter MV110 or MV145, in compact set-up or separate version with connecting cable. The sensor has a steel body with connecting flanges, one pair of excitation coils and electrodes; available liners: PTFE (DN25–DN350, water up to 90 °C), polypropylene (DN25–DN150, 50 °C), Rilsan (DN50–DN350, 50 °C) and Ebonite (DN200–DN350, 50 °C). The converter provides an LCD display, impulse and current outputs, and non-legally relevant communication via RS485 or USB mini-B.\"\n\nThat's a reasonable summary.\n\nNow config_level details:\n\n1. {\n  \"attribute\": \"ratio_q3_over_q1\",\n  \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"class 1\", \"value\": 250},\n    {\"condition\": \"class 2\", \"value\": 400}\n  ]\n}\nDimensionless.\n\n2. mpe lower:\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_permissible_error_lower_zone\",\n  \"axis\": \"accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"class 1\", \"value\": -3? } \n  ]\n}\n\nHmm, ±3%: I'll use value string \"±3\" with unit \"%\" — wait, but rule 2 says numbers not strings where possible. ± can be represented... The MPE is ±3% meaning ±3% error. I could write value: \"±3%\" — as a string because of the ±. Or value: 3 with unit \"%\" and a \"sign\": \"±\"? Schema fixed: {condition, value, unit_symbol}. I'll use \"value\": \"±3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"%\". I think that's acceptable (the ± makes it non-pure-numeric). Many examples in these tasks use strings for such.\n\n3. mpe upper (axis \"accuracy_class_and_water_temperature\"):\nconditions:\n- \"class 1, water temperature ≤ 30 °C\" → ±1 %\n- \"class 2, water temperature ≤ 30 °C\" → ±2 %\n- \"class 1, water temperature > 30 °C\" → ±2 %\n- \"class 2, water temperature > 30 °C\" → ±3 %\n\n4. q1 (axis \"nominal_diameter_and_accuracy_class\"):\n26 entries: condition \"DN25, class 1 (Q3/Q1=250)\" value 0.064 unit m³/h; \"DN25, class 2 (Q3/Q1=400)\" 0.040; etc.\n\n5. q2 same: 26 entries.\n\n6. maximum_admissible_temperature (axis \"liner_material\"):\n- PTFE: 90 °C\n- Polypropylene: 50 °C\n- Rilsan: 50 °C\n- Ebonite: 50 °C\n\n7. temperature_class (axis \"liner_material\"):\n- PTFE: \"T90, T50, T30\"\n- Polypropylene: \"T50, T30\"\n- Rilsan: \"T50, T30 (from DN50)\"\n- Ebonite: \"T50, T30 (from DN200)\"\n\n8. power_supply_voltage (axis \"module_variant\"):\n- \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\": {min:100, max:240} V\n- \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\": {min:24, max:36} V\n- \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\": {min:10, max:48} V\n- \"MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery\": {min:100, max:240} V\n- \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery\": {min:10, max:48} V\n- \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\": {min:10, max:48} V\n\n9. power_supply_type (axis \"module_variant\"):\n- HV: \"AC\"; LV: \"AC/DC\"; LLV: \"DC\"; MV145 HV: \"AC\"; MV145 LLV+alk: \"DC\"; MV145 LLV+lit: \"DC\"\n\n10. power_supply_frequency (axis \"module_variant\"):\n- MV110 HV: {min:45,max:66} Hz\n- MV110 LV: {min:45,max:66} Hz\n- MV110 LLV: null (DC supply, no frequency)\n- MV145 HV+lit: {min:45,max:66} Hz\n- MV145 LLV+alk: null\n- MV145 LLV+lit: null\n\nHmm, for MV145 LLV+alk the cell is blank (OCR empty) and LLV+lit is \"-\". Both effectively n/a for DC. I'll set null with the same caveat. Fine.\n\n11. internal_backup_source (axis \"module_variant\"):\n- MV110 HV: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\"\n- MV110 LV: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\"\n- MV110 LLV: null\n- MV145 HV + lit. battery: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh lithium battery\"\n- MV145 LLV + alk. battery: \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh alkaline battery\"\n- MV145 LLV + lit. battery: null\n\nPage 3 says MV145 HV + lit: \"internal backup source 3.7V DC lithium battery\"; LLV + alk: \"3.7V DC alka. battery\"; LLV + lit: \"3.7V DC lithium. battery\" — wait, LLV + lit battery has NO mains backup? Page 3: \"The water meter MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit.. battery has power supply by main 10 – 48V DC with internal backup source 3.7V DC lithium. battery.\" But the table says \"-\" for MV145 LLV+lit internal backup. Conflict! Page 12 table: MV145 row \"Internal backup source\": 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery | 3.7V DC 5200mAh battery | \"-\". So per table, LLV+lit has no internal backup (the \"lit. battery\" in its name refers to... hmm, its main battery is lithium?). Actually for LLV+lit, the main supply is battery powered (10-48V DC) with lithium battery? Confusing. I'll follow the table (page 12): null for LLV+lit. And for MV110 HV/LV the table shows 3.7V backup even though page 3 doesn't mention. Follow the table.\n\n12. software_version (axis \"electronics\"):\n- MV110: \"V.1.04.0005.0004\"\n- MV145: \"V.1.05.0004.0005\"\n\n13. crc_checksum (axis \"electronics\"):\n- MV110: \"9BA34F3B\"\n- MV145: \"C96453B1\"\n\nAlso cut_off 0.2% and empty pipe detection \"on\" — type_level (same for both electronics).\n\nmodel_level entries:\n\n1. {\"attribute\": \"transitional_flowrate_q3\", \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\", \"values\": [{\"model\": \"DN25\", \"value\": 16}, {\"model\": \"DN32\", \"value\": 25}, {\"model\": \"DN40\", \"value\": 40}, {\"model\": \"DN50\", \"value\": 63}, {\"model\": \"DN65\", \"value\": 100}, {\"model\": \"DN80\", \"value\": 160}, {\"model\": \"DN100\", \"value\": 250}, {\"model\": \"DN125\", \"value\": 400}, {\"model\": \"DN150\", \"value\": 630}, {\"model\": \"DN200\", \"value\": 1000}, {\"model\": \"DN250\", \"value\": 1600}, {\"model\": \"DN300\", \"value\": 1600}, {\"model\": \"DN350\", \"value\": 1600}]}\n\nQ3 is \"transitional flowrate\" in R49 (Q3 is the permanent flowrate actually — in R49, Q3 is the flowrate... Q3 is \"permanent flowrate\"? For water meters: Q1 minimum, Q2 transitional, Q3 permanent/nominal, Q4 maximum). Yes: R49 uses Q3 as permanent flowrate, Q4 maximum. So:\n- q1 minimum flowrate\n- q2 transitional flowrate\n- q3 permanent flowrate\n- q4 maximum flowrate\n\nAttribute names: minimum_flowrate_q1, transitional_flowrate_q2, permanent_flowrate_q3, maximum_flowrate_q4.\n\n2. maximum_flowrate_q4: 20, 31.25, 50, 73, 125, 200, 313, 500, 780, 1250, 2000, 2000, 2000.\n\n3. indicating_range: 99999, 99999, 99999, 99999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 999999, 9999999, 9999999, 9999999, 9999999 (m³)\n\n4. resolution_of_indicating_device: 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.0001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.001, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01, 0.01 (m³)\n\n5. length: 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 200, 250, 250, 300, 350, 450, 500, 550 (mm)\n\nAlso accuracy class per model? Both classes available for all DNs, so type_level: accuracy_class \"1, 2\".\n\nAlso should model_level include q1/q2? No, they're in config_level (2D).\n\nHmm — wait. Maybe better to include the model attribute \"accuracy_class_and_ratio\" per model in models[].attributes: {\"accuracy_classes\": [{\"class\": 1, \"ratio\": 250}, {\"class\": 2, \"ratio\": 400}]}. That duplicates. Skip; config entry #1 covers ratio-class mapping.\n\nmatrix_tables:\n\nTable 1: \"Flow characteristics by nominal diameter\"\ncolumns: [\"nominal_diameter\", \"q1_class1_r250\", \"q2_class1_r250\", \"q1_class2_r400\", \"q2_class2_r400\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"length\", \"indicating_range\", \"indicating_resolution\"]\ncolumn_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"mm\", \"m³\", \"m³\"]\nrows: 13 objects:\n{\"nominal_diameter\": 25, \"q1_class1_r250\": 0.064, \"q2_class1_r250\": 0.102, \"q1_class2_r400\": 0.040, \"q2_class2_r400\": 0.064, \"q3\": 16, \"q4\": 20, \"length\": 200, \"indicating_range\": 99999, \"indicating_resolution\": 0.0001}\n... DN350: {\"nominal_diameter\": 350, ..., \"q3\": 1600, \"q4\": 2000, \"length\": 550, \"indicating_range\": 9999999, \"indicating_resolution\": 0.01}\ndescription: \"Values per nominal diameter; class 1 variant has Q3/Q1 = 250, class 2 variant has Q3/Q1 = 400. Q2/Q1 = 1.6 and Q4/Q3 = 1.25 for all sizes. DN200–DN350 available with converter MV110 only.\"\n\nTable 2: \"Power supply and electrical specification\"\ncolumns: [\"module_designation\", \"electronics\", \"supply_type\", \"supply_voltage\", \"frequency\", \"internal_backup_source\", \"minimum_battery_life_years\", \"software_version\", \"crc_checksum\", \"cut_off\", \"empty_pipe_detection\"]\nRows:\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 HV\", \"electronics\": \"MV110\", \"supply_type\": \"AC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"100–240 V\", \"frequency\": \"45–66 Hz\", \"internal_backup_source\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\", \"minimum_battery_life_years\": null, \"software_version\": \"V.1.04.0005.0004\", \"crc_checksum\": \"9BA34F3B\", \"cut_off\": \"0.2 %\", \"empty_pipe_detection\": \"on\"}\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 LV\", \"electronics\": \"MV110\", \"supply_type\": \"AC/DC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"24–36 V\", \"frequency\": \"45–66 Hz\", \"internal_backup_source\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\", ...}\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV110 LLV\", \"electronics\": \"MV110\", \"supply_type\": \"DC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"10–48 V\", \"frequency\": null, \"internal_backup_source\": null, ...}\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV145 HV + lit. battery\", \"electronics\": \"MV145\", \"supply_type\": \"AC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"100–240 V\", \"frequency\": \"45–66 Hz\", \"internal_backup_source\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\", \"software_version\": \"V.1.05.0004.0005\", \"crc_checksum\": \"C96453B1\", ...}\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + alk. battery\", \"electronics\": \"MV145\", \"supply_type\": \"DC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"10–48 V\", \"frequency\": null, \"internal_backup_source\": \"3.7 V DC; 5200 mAh battery\", ...}\n{\"module_designation\": \"MS2500 / MV145 LLV + lit. battery\", \"electronics\": \"MV145\", \"supply_type\": \"DC\", \"supply_voltage\": \"10–48 V\", \"frequency\": null, \"internal_backup_source\": null, ...}\n\nDescription: \"MV110 software version V.1.04.0005.0004 (CRC 9BA34F3B); MV145 software version V.1.05.0004.0005 (CRC C96453B1). Cut-off 0.2 % and empty pipe detection on for all variants.\" Keep rows explicit anyway.\n\nHmm wait, in table 2 rows for MV145 HV, the voltage uses \"–\" en-dash; fine.\n\ncomponents:\n\n[\n  {\n    \"role\": \"sensor (measurement transducer)\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MS2500\"],\n    \"alternatives\": null,\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"construction\": \"steel body with connecting flanges, one pair of excitation coils, electrodes and connection to the converter\",\n      \"liner_options\": [\n        {\"material\": \"PTFE\", \"nominal_diameters\": \"DN25–DN350\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 90, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}},\n        {\"material\": \"Polypropylene\", \"nominal_diameters\": \"DN25–DN150\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}},\n        {\"material\": \"Rilsan\", \"nominal_diameters\": \"DN50–DN350\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}},\n        {\"material\": \"Ebonite\", \"nominal_diameters\": \"DN200–DN350\", \"max_water_temperature\": {\"value\": 50, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}}\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  {\n    \"role\": \"converter with electronic indicating device\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"],\n    \"alternatives\": \"OR\",\n    \"characteristics\": {\n      \"display\": \"LCD showing volume and flow\",\n      \"volume_display_resolution\": {\"value\": 0.001, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³\"},\n      \"controls\": \"three buttons\",\n      \"outputs\": [\"impulse output\", \"current output\"],\n      \"non_legally_relevant_communication\": [\"RS485\", \"USB mini-B\"],\n      \"security\": \"password-protected user settings; legally relevant software separated\"\n    }\n  }\n]\n\nAlternatives for converters: \"MV110 or MV145\" → \"OR\". Good.\n\nAlso compact vs separate version — mention in certified_type.description (already).\n\nNow the \"sealing\" details — probably skip; too granular. Could add to description? Skip.\n\nMarking requirements — skip (not characteristics).\n\nOne more check: \"Installation sensitivity class (Ux Dx) – it does not differ from U0 D0\" → type_level installation_sensitivity_class {\"value\": \"U0 D0\"} plus flow_profile_sensitivity_class \"U0D0\". Both same. I'll include both as given.\n\nenvironmental_class \"O (-25 to +55 °C)\" — I'll do:\n- \"environmental_class\": {\"value\": \"O\"}\n- \"environmental_class_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": -25, \"max\": 55}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\"}\n\nMechanical class B, O.\n\nNow certificate.number: include revision? \"R49/2013-A-CZ1-2022.02\" as printed. Good.\n\noiml_issuer_id: The certificate no. contains \"CZ1\". Some certs have explicit issuer IDs (e.g., \"CMI\" ID). Not present explicitly → omit.\n\ndate_issued: 2022-09-13.\n\nAlso there's \"Verze 20-001\" (form version) — skip.\n\nPage total 15.\n\nLet me also double check \"Accuracy class\" pairing with R ratio once more from page 4: \nRow Q3/Q1: 250 | 400 | 250 | 400 | 250 | 400.\nRow Accuracy class: 1 | 2 | 1 | 2 | 1 | 2.\nSo column 1: class 1, R250, Q1=0.064, Q2=0.102, Q3=16, Q4=20.\nColumn 2: class 2, R400, Q1=0.040, Q2=0.064.\n\nHmm wait, that's odd: class 1 with R250 and class 2 with R400? Usually higher R (400) is better metrology (class 1?). But whatever — R49 doesn't link class to R ratio. The pairing is as the table shows. Record it.\n\nActually hold on, is it possible that the accuracy class row simply indicates both classes 1 and 2 apply across, and the Q3/Q1 row independently lists both ratios? The column alignment suggests 1↔250 and 2↔400. I'll encode conditions as \"class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\" for q1/q2 config values, which is faithful to alignment.\n\nNow q1 for DN32 R400: 0.062 (Q3/R = 25/400 = 0.0625 ✓). DN25 R400: 16/400 = 0.04 ✓. DN40: 40/400 = 0.1 ✓. DN50: 63/400 = 0.1575 ≈ 0.158 ✓. DN65: 100/400 = 0.25 ✓. DN80: 0.4 ✓. DN100: 250/400 = 0.625 ≈ 0.63 ✓. DN125: 1.0 ✓. DN150: 1.575 ≈ 1.60 ✓. DN200: 2.5 ✓. DN250: 4.0 ✓. \n\nq2 = 1.6 × q1: DN150 R400: 1.6×1.6 = 2.56 but printed 1.56. I'll keep 1.56 (as printed). Actually, hmm — should I \"fix\" it? No. Record as printed. Maybe add nothing.\n\nq4 DN50: 73.0 — as printed (1.25×63=78.75, but printed 73.0). Keep 73.\n\nIndicating range DN40: 99 999 m³ — fine.\n\nNow writing model entries with attributes for converter availability:\n\nmodels:\n[{\"model_id\": \"DN25\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN32\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN40\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN50\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN65\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN80\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN100\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN125\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN150\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\", \"MV145\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN200\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN250\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN300\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\"]}},\n {\"model_id\": \"DN350\", \"attributes\": {\"converters\": [\"MV110\"]}}]\n\nGood.\n\nType_level list finalize:\n- measuring_principle: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- accuracy_class: \"1, 2\"\n- reverse_flow: \"designed to measure\"\n- pressure_loss_class: \"Δp40\"\n- water_pressure_class: \"MAP16\"\n- maximum_admissible_pressure: 1.6 MPa\n- ratio_q2_over_q1: 1.6\n- ratio_q4_over_q3: 1.25\n- environmental_class: \"O\"\n- environmental_class_temperature_range: {min:-25, max:55} °C\n- electromagnetic_environment: \"E1, E2\"\n- mechanical_class: \"B, O\"\n- orientation: \"any\"\n- connection_type: \"Flanged\"\n- minimum_straight_length_of_inlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_of_outlet_pipe: 0 mm\n- flow_profile_sensitivity_class: \"U0D0\"\n- installation_sensitivity_class: \"U0 D0\"\n- flow_conditioner: \"No\"\n- eut_testing_category: \"Electromagnetic water meter\"\n- eut_testing_case: \"Case D\"\n- cut_off: 0.2 %\n- empty_pipe_detection: \"on\"\n\nStructured: each {\"value\": ..., \"unit_symbol\": ...}.\n\nNote rule 1: no footnote markers etc. None here.\n\nAlso \"volume_resolution_displayed\" 0.001 m³ (page 3) — but per-DN resolutions differ; page 3 statement is about digital display resolution 0.001 m³. Per-DN table values are \"Resolution of the indicating device\". Might conflict (DN25 resolution 0.0001). Skip the page 3 one, or include as \"display_volume_resolution\" 0.001? I'll skip to avoid confusion — no wait, components MV converter characteristics has volume_display_resolution 0.001 m³ from page 3 text. Hmm, that may conflict with table. The page-3 sentence: \"The water meter displays the volume resolution of 0.001 m3 on the digital display.\" vs table per-DN resolution 0.0001–0.01. I'll drop it from components to avoid inconsistency. Actually keep? I'll omit it — the table is authoritative.\n\nNow config_level ordering — attribute per entry.\n\nq1 config entry:\n\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"minimum_flowrate_q1\",\n  \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n  \"axis\": \"nominal_diameter_and_accuracy_class\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\", \"value\": 0.064},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN25, class 2 (Q3/Q1 = 400)\", \"value\": 0.040},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN32, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\", \"value\": 0.100},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN32, class 2 (Q3/Q1 = 400)\", \"value\": 0.062},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\", \"value\": 0.160},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN40, class 2 (Q3/Q1 = 400)\", \"value\": 0.100},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN50, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\", \"value\": 0.252},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN50, class 2 (Q3/Q1 = 400)\", \"value\": 0.158},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN65, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 250)\", \"value\": 0.400},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN65, class 2 (Q3/Q1 = 400)\", \"value\": 0.250},\n    {\"condition\": \"DN80, class 1 (Q3/Q1 = 2